  <div class="article-body">

<p>Start with a set of dates.</p>
<p>September 8–11: UNESCO's "Digital Learning Week" was held at its Paris headquarters, themed "Education in the Age of AI." A team from Tsinghua University stood on that stage with an education agent project called OpenMAIC.</p>
<p>September 23: In Beijing, at a State Council Information Office press conference on the "15th Five-Year Plan," China's Ministry of Education called for "advancing the digital-intelligent transformation of education and strengthening AI literacy among teachers and students."</p>
<p>The same day, a report from the "2026 AI Large Model CEO Roundtable" proposed a five-layer AI literacy framework: "know, use, discern, create, be responsible."</p>
<p>September 24: At the Yunqi Conference in Hangzhou, TAL Education's "Jiuzhang Teacher" announced it had launched over 170 teaching agents, with more than 300,000 registered users.</p>
<p>September 24: Across the ocean, at its Connect conference, Meta unveiled "Muse Charm" — a small device that clips onto a keyring and is built to talk with its AI assistant, Muse — and said it plans to bring Muse into smart glasses.</p>
<p>September 25: Microsoft released a new "super app" version of Copilot, adding three capabilities: Home, Code, and Autopilot.</p>
<p>September 28: The Hong Kong Education Bureau's first AI-education teacher-training session began.</p>
<p>In a single month, from Paris to Beijing, from Hangzhou to Silicon Valley, education AI and consumer AI pressed the accelerator almost simultaneously. This is no coincidence.</p>
<p>This article tries to answer one question: <strong>when so many moves happen at once, what exactly are they competing over?</strong></p>
<p>My assessment: they are fighting on two fronts at the same time — <strong>a battle over standards, and a battle over gateways.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2 id="1-china-is-setting-standards-a-chain-from-policy-to-classroom">1. China Is "Setting Standards": A Chain from Policy to Classroom</h2>
<p>First front. China's moves share one trait: <strong>they answer questions like "what counts as AI literacy, how do we teach it, and to what level."</strong></p>
<p><strong>Link one: Policy sets the tone.</strong></p>
<p>At the September 23 press conference, the Ministry of Education's phrase was "advancing the digital-intelligent transformation of education" — note "digital-intelligent," not merely "digital." The two characters point at the AI layer. The emphasis that day: "strengthening AI literacy among teachers and students."</p>
<p>The weight of that sentence: <strong>it elevates "AI literacy" from an optional bonus to a basic capability the education system must deliver.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Link two: A literacy framework lands.</strong></p>
<p>Around the same period, a report from the "2026 AI Large Model CEO Roundtable" proposed a five-layer AI literacy framework: "know, use, discern, create, be responsible."</p>
<p>These five words are a progressive path: first "know" what AI is; then learn to "use" it; then "discern" (judge whether AI is right); then "create"; and finally "responsibility" (understand who bears the consequences of using AI).</p>
<p>One number in the report is worth pondering: among surveyed primary-school students, 97.9% had at least <em>heard of</em> AI, and 74.8% had already encountered AI in daily study. Yet the report's phrasing was "high awareness, shallow understanding."</p>
<p><strong>In plain terms: kids all know AI, but no one is teaching them to truly understand it.</strong> That is exactly the gap the framework aims to close.</p>
<p><strong>Link three: The standard goes international.</strong></p>
<p>On September 8–11, at UNESCO's Digital Learning Week, the MAIC team from Tsinghua University's School of Education used a 90-minute presentation to bring OpenMAIC to international peers.</p>
<p>The significance: <strong>China's practice of "how AI enters education" has begun to enter the international conversation.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Link four: It reaches the classroom.</strong></p>
<p>On September 28, the Hong Kong Education Bureau's first AI-education teacher-training session began (a repeat session was scheduled for October 5). This looks smallest yet matters most — <strong>standards ultimately land on teachers.</strong> If teachers can't use it, the framework stays in a document forever.</p>
<p>Connect the four links and you see a complete chain: <strong>policy tone → framework design → international dialogue → teacher training.</strong> From "should we do this" all the way to "how do teachers teach it."</p>
<p>That is the first layer of the "battle over standards": <strong>whoever defines "competent AI literacy" defines the starting line for the next generation.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2 id="2-industry-is-setting-the-landscape-a-highly-concentrated-market">2. Industry Is "Setting the Landscape": A Highly Concentrated Market</h2>
<p>As standards form, the market is rapidly converging too.</p>
<p><strong>First, one number: 79.4%.</strong></p>
<p>In the first half of 2026, four brands — Zuoyebang, Xueersi, iFLYTEK, and Yuanfudao — together held 79.4% of <strong>unit-sales share</strong> in the learning-machine market, up 11.6 percentage points year on year.</p>
<p>(A note on scope: this is <em>unit-sales</em> share, for the period <em>H1 2026</em>. A separate, differently-scoped figure exists — in Q1 2026, six major learning-tablet brands together held 87.5%. The two should not be conflated.)</p>
<p><strong>What does 79.4% mean?</strong> It means the market is already highly concentrated: four players take nearly 80%, and everyone else splits the remaining 20%.</p>
<p><strong>Now a case: Jiuzhang Teacher.</strong></p>
<p>On September 24 at the Yunqi Conference, TAL Education's "Jiuzhang AiXue Teacher Edition" (abbreviated "Jiuzhang Teacher") announced: over 170 teaching agents launched, covering the full teaching cycle — lesson prep, question-setting, homework grading, learning diagnostics, and parent communication — with more than 300,000 registered users.</p>
<p>Its positioning was stated clearly: "the professional teaching-research group for teachers in the AI era." It does not face students directly; it <strong>serves teachers</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Together, these two numbers reveal two features of China's education AI:</strong></p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>The market is highly concentrated.</strong> The top four taking 80% signals a shift from "land-grab" to "consolidation of the top."</li>
<li><strong>Product form has shifted to "agents."</strong> From "an app" to "a cluster of agents covering the full teaching cycle."</li>
</ol>
<p>The market also repeats a narrative — "AI learning machines to exceed 100 billion yuan in scale in 2026." To be clear: <strong>this is a forecast, not a fact.</strong> And the "100 billion" scope is usually the <em>education smart-hardware</em> category as a whole (including AI learning machines), not AI learning machines alone.</p>
<p><strong>So the essence of the second front: it is not one product winning — it is an ecosystem taking shape.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2 id="3-global-giants-are-seizing-gateways-ai-is-becoming-an-operating-system">3. Global Giants Are "Seizing Gateways": AI Is Becoming an Operating System</h2>
<p>If China's moves concentrate on "standards" and "ecosystem," the global giants' keyword is another one: <strong>gateway.</strong></p>
<p><strong>First: Microsoft's Copilot "super app."</strong></p>
<p>On September 25, Microsoft released a new Copilot adding three capabilities: Home, Code, and Autopilot.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Home:</strong> an aggregation page that gathers your email and other information in one place;</li>
<li><strong>Code:</strong> oriented toward programming;</li>
<li><strong>Autopilot:</strong> lets AI not merely "chat," but continuously and autonomously execute tasks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Microsoft's positioning was blunt: to extend the AI assistant from a "chat tool" into "an intelligent agent that can execute tasks, build applications, and work continuously."</p>
<p><strong>What is the essence of this move?</strong> It turns AI from a tool "you open when you need it" into <strong>an interface you live and work inside every day.</strong></p>
<p><strong>Second: Meta's Muse and Muse Charm.</strong></p>
<p>In the same days, at its Connect conference, Meta announced Muse Charm — a small wearable device built around its AI assistant, Muse. The plan: bring Muse into smart glasses within the next few months, so a user wearing the glasses can talk directly to Muse about whatever they see.</p>
<p>(Media nicknamed it an "AI pet," but its real positioning is <strong>a wearable hardware gateway to the Muse agent.</strong>)</p>
<p><strong>What is the essence of this move?</strong> It shifts AI's gateway from "screen" to "wearable" — no longer "look down at your phone to use AI," but "AI is right in front of your eyes."</p>
<p><strong>Put the two together and it becomes clear:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Microsoft is fighting for the <strong>workspace</strong> gateway;</li>
<li>Meta is fighting for the <strong>wearable space</strong> gateway.</li>
</ul>
<p>Their shared ambition is the same: <strong>to make AI the thing you open first and keep open always.</strong> Like today's operating system — you don't "remember to open Windows"; you're always inside it.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="4-where-the-two-fronts-meet">4. Where the Two Fronts Meet</h2>
<p>By now, each front is clear. But they actually converge at one point.</p>
<p><strong>"Standards" teach people <em>how to use AI</em>; "gateways" contest <em>the first AI people open</em>.</strong></p>
<p>These two seem unrelated but are causally linked:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Whoever holds the gateway gets to turn their usage into the standard.</strong> Consider: if a hundred million people work inside some Copilot every day, then "what professional AI use looks like" will be defined by that product.</li>
<li><strong>Whoever sets the standard can legitimize tools that fit it.</strong> A "five-layer literacy framework" ultimately becomes curricula, exams, even procurement standards — and products that fit it gain a head start.</li>
</ul>
<p>So this is not a division of labor where "China does education, America does products." <strong>Both sides' moves ultimately point at the same goal: defining how the next generation defaults to living with AI.</strong></p>
<p><strong>China's path is "top-down":</strong> set standards first, then push into classrooms, using policy power to spread the literacy framework.</p>
<p><strong>Global giants' path is "bottom-up":</strong> seize the gateway first, let habits form through use, and let habit gradually become de facto standard.</p>
<p>Which path wins? Too early to say. But one thing is certain: <strong>they will meet.</strong> When China's literacy standards meet the global giants' product gateways, the question "who defines AI literacy" becomes very concrete.</p>
<hr />
<h2 id="5-three-questions-for-readers">5. Three Questions for Readers</h2>
<p>As an ordinary person, what do these grand competitions have to do with you? Quite a lot. Because they will ultimately determine <strong>what your children learn, use, and are judged by at school.</strong></p>
<p>So when you next see "education AI" news, don't just look at "what new product came out." Ask three questions first:</p>
<p><strong>Question one: Who is setting the standard?</strong> — Policy, a framework, or some product's usage habit? Whoever sets the standard gets the outcome leaning their way.</p>
<p><strong>Question two: Who is holding the gateway?</strong> — A phone, a computer, or glasses and wearables? Whoever holds "the first AI you open" holds default authority.</p>
<p><strong>Question three: Is my (or my child's) "literacy" sufficient?</strong> — Not "can you use the tool," but "can you judge, can you create, can you take responsibility." That is exactly what "know, use, discern, create, be responsible" answers.</p>
<p><strong>Think through these three, and you can basically judge: is a piece of education-AI news "real progress," or "a passing trend"?</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2 id="closing">Closing</h2>
<p>Back to that timetable.</p>
<p>From September 8 to September 28 — just three weeks — we saw moves across six dimensions at once: policy, standards, academia, industry, hardware, software. Such density is no coincidence; it is a signal.</p>
<p><strong>It signals that education AI is moving from the stage of "whether it exists" into the stage of "who gets to decide."</strong></p>
<p>And that "who gets to decide" ultimately answers not a technical question, but an older one: <strong>what kind of people do we want to cultivate?</strong></p>
<p>Tools will keep updating; gateways will keep migrating. But if "literacy" is done solidly — <strong>if the next generation knows what AI is, can use it, can discern it, can create with it, and is willing to take responsibility for using it</strong> — then no matter how gateways change or standards are contested, the human remains the one making the call.</p>
<p>That is probably the answer every standard and every product ultimately cannot avoid.</p>
